    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ahmadiyya is an Islamic messianic movement that arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in the late 19th century. Its founder, Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (1835–1908) of Qadian, said he had been divinely appointed as both the promised Messiah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> whom Muslims expect towards the end times, to bring about by peaceful means the final triumph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. At the end of the 19th century he proclaimed himself the Centennial Reformer of Islam (Mujaddid) and gained a considerable following, especially in the United Provinces, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. He and his followers believe that Muhammad and many other scriptures foretold his coming.</p><p>Formally, the Community's history begins on 23 March 1889, when Ahmad took the oath of allegiance from a number of companions at a home in Ludhiana. The movement emerged within Islam and also in response to the Christian and Arya Samaj missionary activity that was widespread in the 19th century. Ahmadis trace its history further back, to the early life of Ahmad, who reportedly began receiving revelations about his future, and to the traditions of various world religions.</p><p>The name came about a decade after the founding. In a manifesto dated 4 November 1900, Ahmad chose it with reference to Muhammad's alternative name Ahmad, and deemed it a blameworthy innovation to name an Islamic school after anyone but Muhammad.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ahmadis hold that the six articles of faith and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-pillars-of-islam">Five Pillars of Islam</a> are the basis of their belief and practice, identical to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims. They accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> as their holy text, face the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in prayer, follow the sunnah and accept the authority of the ahadith and of the four Rightly Guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. In jurisprudence they reject strict adherence to any one school, giving first place to the Quran and sunnah, though they usually base rulings on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> method where these sources are not clear. They believe in the absolute unity of God, and that all major world religions have divine origins and form part of a plan towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the final religion.</p><p>Several teachings are distinct. Ahmadis understand Khatam an-Nabiyyin differently from most Muslims: they hold that Muhammad was the last law-bearing prophet, but that new prophets wholly subordinate to him may still come. They regard Mirza Ghulam Ahmad as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> and the promised Messiah. They believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> survived the crucifixion and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, that no verse of the Quran abrogates another, and that hell is a temporary abode in which souls are cleansed.</p><p>Ahmadis divide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> into three kinds: the struggle against the self, the peaceful propagation of Islam, and armed struggle, permitted only in self-defence against extreme persecution and then only on the Caliph's instruction. They also hold that religion and science cannot conflict.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> is the holy text of the Ahmadiyya, and Ahmadis accept the sunnah and the authority of the ahadith, using the Kutub al-Sittah, the six canonical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> collections. They hold that the Quran is the final divine scripture and that its teachings are timeless; with the exception of the Quran, they believe the earlier scriptures are open to human interpolation. According to Ahmadi teaching, books outside the Abrahamic tradition, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a>, are also of divine origin.</p><p>The writings of Mirza Ghulam Ahmad hold a central place in the Community. The third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> oversaw the compilation of his dialogues and sayings, and directed the complete collection of his dreams, visions and verbal revelations. In one passage he gives his summary of the faith: to love God and to love humankind so much that one counts the suffering of others as one's own.</p><p>Later Caliphs added to this literature. The second Caliph, Mirza Basheer-ud-Din Mahmood Ahmad, wrote a ten-volume commentary on the Quran, and the Tehrik-e-Jadid scheme he launched produced a large body of writing in defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and of Ahmadiyya beliefs. The fourth Caliph wrote many books, among them Murder in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> and Revelation, Rationality, Knowledge and Truth. Under the first Caliph the Community oversaw an English translation of the Quran, and it has since translated the Quran into over 70 languages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement drew its first followers in the United Provinces, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. Overseas missions began on an organised level as early as 1913, with the mission at Putney, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and for many nations the Ahmadiyya was the first contact with preachers from the Muslim world. According to Richard Brent Turner, until the mid-1950s it was arguably the most influential community in African-American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>Under the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a>, whose term lasted from 1914 to 1965, missions were established in 46 countries, mosques were built abroad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was published in several major languages. His Tehrik-e-Jadid scheme financed the training and dispatch of missionaries outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. The third Caliph, Mirza Nasir Ahmad, extended the work, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: after a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in 1970 he set up the Nusrat Jehan Scheme, which now runs many medical facilities and schools there. In the post-colonial era the Community is credited with much of the spread of Islam in Africa.</p><p>By 2016 the Community had been established in 209 countries and territories, with concentrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, West Africa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It is a minority Muslim group in almost every country. In 2001 the World Christian Encyclopedia estimated that it was the fastest growing group within Islam.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Community is led by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a>, whom Ahmadis regard as the resumption of the Rightly Guided Caliphate. They hold that a caliph is designated primarily by God, who guides the electors through visions, dreams and spiritual guidance; no campaigning is permitted. The office is above all spiritual, and the Caliph is also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>, Leader of the Faithful. The Majlis-ash-Shura, the Consultative Council founded in 1922, advises him, but he need not accept its views. The current and fifth Caliph is Mirza Masroor Ahmad, elected in 2003.</p><p>The Community is organised into national, regional and local communities, each with a president, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> at the national level. Five auxiliary organisations train members by age and sex, the largest being the Lajna Ima'illah for women over 15. Jamia al-Ahmadiyya, founded in 1906, trains missionaries, and the Jalsa Salana, an annual convention, is the most important function.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> the second Caliph founded the city of Rabwah, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, on 20 September 1948. The Community regards Qadian as its spiritual headquarters. The fourth Caliph moved to the Fazl Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1984, and the fifth moved the headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a>, Tilford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in 2019. Dissent over the nature of Ahmad's prophethood and his succession led in 1914 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> Ahmadiyya Movement, which has claimed 30,000 adherents.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mainstream Muslims, who believe that Muhammad was the final prophet, have characterised the recognition of Ahmad as a prophet as heretical, and some regard Ahmadis as infidels. The Community says it has been subject to persecution and discrimination since 1889. In 1973 the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation declared that the Ahmadiyya was not linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the government has declared Ahmadis non-Muslims. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims use the terms Qadiyani and Mirzai for Ahmadis, which are names given by others that the Community never uses of itself.</p><p>In 1984 Pakistan promulgated Ordinance XX, which jeopardised the safety of the Community and made it impossible for the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> to perform his duties, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Pakistan officially claims 290,000 Ahmadis, while Pakistani Ahmadis have boycotted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> since 1974; independent estimates run from 2 million to 5 million.</p><p>The Community has built over 15,000 mosques, over 500 schools and over 30 hospitals through its missionary council. It launched Muslim Television Ahmadiyya, the first Muslim satellite television network, and the charity Humanity First. Its motto, coined by the third Caliph, is love for all, hatred for none. Abdus Salam, a Nobel laureate and devout Ahmadi, is among its members.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ahmadiyya Muslim Jama'at began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1889 under Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, whom its members hold to be the promised Messiah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. Led by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> and active in missions worldwide, it is regarded by many Muslims as outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> it has been declared non-Muslim by law.</em></p>

